<h2 id="glossary">Glossary</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>BPMN</strong> — standard process-modeling notation</li>
<li><strong>RPA</strong> — bots mimicking human UI actions</li>
<li><strong>Idempotent</strong> — safe to repeat without duplication</li>
<li><strong>Dead-letter queue</strong> — holding area for failed messages</li>
<li><strong>Swimlane</strong> — BPMN role partition</li>
<li><strong>Straight-through processing</strong> — fully automatic, no human touch</li>
<li><strong>Human-in-the-loop</strong> — AI recommends, human decides</li>
<li><strong>Process mining</strong> — discovering processes from event logs</li>
<li><strong>IDP</strong> — intelligent document processing</li>
<li><strong>Payback period</strong> — months to recover automation cost</li>
<li><strong>Shadow IT</strong> — ungoverned tech built outside IT</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2 id="practice-exercises">Practice Exercises</h2>
<ol>
<li>Map a process from your life (e.g., expense claims) in basic BPMN. Identify two waste steps.</li>
<li>Score three automation candidates with the 5-factor matrix. Which wins and why?</li>
<li>For one process, justify the tool choice per step (API/workflow/RPA/code).</li>
<li>Design idempotency for a payment automation. What key do you use?</li>
<li>Write the error-handling plan (retry, dead-letter, alert, fallback) for a sync automation.</li>
<li>Compute ROI for an automation you propose, with real-ish numbers and maintenance.</li>
<li>Plan the change management for automating a colleague's task. What are the three risks?</li>
<li>Compare two workflow platforms on 3-year TCO for 1M executions/month.</li>
<li>When is RPA the wrong tool? Give a brittleness-budget argument with numbers.</li>
<li>Draft the evaluation section of your automation paper: metrics, baselines, follow-up period.</li>
</ol>
